17] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353140 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353471 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353780 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186352605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354235 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f0f1dab6-f8cf-5d41-b492-b3aa875e7c0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354235 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}<25.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "653d874d-a486-580e-a2d8-67e03055d454" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354597 #edb/ref 1074 "Isolating the exponential term, we find\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\cdot {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <25 \\\\ {(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x} & <5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, taking the natural logarithm of both sides and applying the laws of logarithms, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{ln}⁡[{(\\frac{4}{9})}^{x}] & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{4}{9}) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5 \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9) & <\\operatorname{ln}⁡5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, note that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9$ is a negative quantity, so when we divide both sides by it, we need to flip the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & >\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡9}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186354200 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1069 "q-87876" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1071 "Solve the inequality $-4-{e}^{3x}>2.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48ad6fff-2c0d-5121-b124-5aea1ae6b3d8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354610 #edb/ref 1074 "First, we isolate the exponential term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4-{e}^{3x} & >2 \\\\ -{e}^{3x} & >6 \\\\ {e}^{3x} & <-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, recall that a positive number raised to any real power is always positive. Since $e>0,$ we have\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}>0\n$$\n\nfor every real value of $x.$\n\nTherefore, ${e}^{3x}$ cannot be less than $-6.$ Hence, the inequality\n\n$$\n{e}^{3x}<-6\n$$\n\nhas no solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428783 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428783 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 2857->1473. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428783 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186050375 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428784 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428784 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source acti